index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..3a1070b32922dac347c6a1f25b1968b53b7f3c10
--- /dev/null
+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,166 @@
+# Induced matching generator for arXiv:1406.2440
+
+> **Status:** locally verified Track B candidate; **not yet shippable**. The
+> required oracle run reached OpenRouter but received HTTP 403 `Key limit
+> exceeded` on every redraw, so STEP 4 and G9 have no scored attempts. The
+> error-only transcript is preserved rather than counted as model failures.
+
+| profile field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — an efficient exact recovery algorithm is disclosed |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | graph |
+| Certificate | integer tuple encoding pendant edges |
+| Intended intuition | change of variables via a translation-equivariant cyclic run |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+## The problem and why the checker is trustworthy
+
+The source is Felix Joos, [*Induced Matchings in Graphs of Bounded Maximum
+Degree*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1406.2440). Section 1 defines an induced matching
+as vertex-disjoint edges with no additional graph edge joining endpoints of two
+chosen edges. The generator gives a bounded-degree graph succinctly: core
+vertices are arranged in clique groups over `Z/pZ`, cross-group edges are
+listed by forbidden differences, and every core vertex has one private leaf.
+The solver returns one pendant edge from every group. `verify` checks the shape,
+residue bounds, and every cross adjacency with exact integer modular arithmetic;
+it never reads `inst["answer"]` and accepts any valid matching of the required
+form.
+
+The construction follows the paper's native pendant-core viewpoint. Section 1
+uses graphs made by attaching pendant vertices to a core both in its sharpness
+examples and in its large-girth discussion. Here the generator first samples
+core coordinates `a_i`. For every pair of groups it independently samples a
+regular forbidden half-set `B_ij` with `0` absent, then translates it by
+`a_j-a_i`. Consequently the core vertices `(i,a_i)` are independent, and their
+private leaf edges are an induced matching known before the graph is assembled.
+Every core vertex has the same degree, so plants and decoys come from the same
+per-vertex degree distribution.
+
+## Why Track B, not Track A
+
+The paper itself prevents a Track A claim for its theorem-native search task.
+Theorem 1 applies to graphs without isolated vertices at maximum degree at least
+1000, and the paragraph before Section 2 explicitly says its constructive proof
+yields a polynomial-time algorithm for the guaranteed matching. Section 1 also
+mentions NP-hardness of finding a *maximum* induced matching in bipartite
+subcubic graphs, but that worst-case optimum result says nothing about this
+inverse-generated distribution.
+
+This module therefore makes the narrower Track B claim. Each unshifted
+`B_ij` has one uniquely longest cyclic occupied run with a common phase. A
+reference algorithm scans all `p` positions of all pair sets, derives the common
+phase from one triangle, and recovers coordinates. It costs `O(k^2 p)`; at the
+provisional hard preset it used **15,497 exact membership operations**, averaging
+about **0.0111 s** over eight seeds. The compact route visually locates the run
+only in the star sets, obtains the phase from a triangle, and uses **138 modular
+operations including final checks**. That route still has to be recognized and
+transcribed without a tool. Whether it actually defeats the four-vendor oracle
+pool remains unmeasured because of the API-key limit.
+
+## Worked demo
+
+For `make_instance(n=19, groups=3, seed=7)`, the rendered graph has 114 vertices,
+1,083 edges, maximum degree 37, and these cross-group sets:
+
+```text
+F 0 1: 0,2,3,4,5,7,8,13,17
+F 0 2: 0,1,5,8,12,14,15,16,17
+F 1 2: 0,2,7,11,13,14,15,16,18
+```
+
+The answer is `[[0,10],[1,11],[2,4]]`.
+
+```python
+>>> verify(inst, [[0, 10], [1, 11], [2, 4]])
+(True, 'ok')
+>>> verify(inst, [[0, 10], [1, 10], [2, 4]])
+(False, 'core endpoints in groups 0 and 1 are joined (forbidden difference 0)')
+```
+
+This smallest setting is hand-solvable: there are only three short residue
+lists, and the marked runs expose the three relative shifts.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | `p` | groups / answer edges | vertices | edges | max degree | rendered chars |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
+| demo | 19 | 3 | 114 | 1,083 | 37 | 1,427 |
+| easy | 43 | 16 | 1,376 | 123,496 | 358 | 9,240 |
+| medium | 83 | 16 | 2,656 | 464,136 | 698 | 16,462 |
+| hard (provisional shipping) | 127 | 16 | 4,064 | 1,090,168 | 1,072 | 25,977 |
+
+Only the modulus and graph haystack grow across the non-demo ladder; the answer
+stays at 16 edges. A final shipping preset cannot be claimed until `harden.py`
+gets three scored failures at one rung.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | local result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 planted witnesses verified |
+| G2 | drop, swap, duplicate, empty, and out-of-range corruptions rejected with 5 distinct reasons |
+| G3 | realistic fenced JSON round-trip passed; answer is JSON-native |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 structure-aware random candidates valid; declared space is `127^16` (112 bits) |
+| G5 | shipping density 0/200,000; demo has 988 valid answers among 6,859; reference cost 15,497 operations |
+| G6 | degree, greedy, 256-restart, and incomplete-run attacks each succeeded 0/8; reference recovery succeeded 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubled modulus 257 verifies with the same 16-edge answer length |
+| G8 | 20/20 composed relabellings invariant, 20/20 carried witnesses valid, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 126 chars, about 32 tokens, 32 atomic elements, 138 intended exact operations |
+| G9(b) | **not run: OpenRouter HTTP 403 key limit** |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 arms
+
+| run | preset | scored solved/attempts | result |
+|---|---|---:|---|
+| bare | easy | 0/0 | four error records, all HTTP 403; no verdict |
+| hinted | — | 0/0 | not run |
+| placebo | — | 0/0 | not run |
+
+`hinted - placebo` is undefined until both arms have scored attempts. The
+structural hint names only the invariant: “Each cross-group forbidden set has
+one uniquely longest translation-equivariant cyclic run.” It does not give the
+phase relation or recovery steps.
+
+## Use
+
+From this directory:
+
+```python
+import random
+import gen_1406_2440 as gen
+
+inst = gen.make_instance(**gen.DIFFICULTY["demo"], seed=7)
+print(gen.render(inst))
+candidate = gen.parse_answer('<answer>[[0,10],[1,11],[2,4]]</answer>')
+print(gen.verify(inst, candidate))
+print(gen.random_candidate(inst, random.Random(1)))
+```
+
+After the oracle evidence exists, emit from the repository root with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 1406.2440 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The measured random prior is uniform over one residue per required group. It
+already enforces the obvious answer shape, but it is not a model of a solver
+that detects the cyclic marker; the zero-hit estimate establishes guess
+resistance, not computational hardness. The promise-specific marker algorithm
+solves every instance quickly and is intentionally disclosed. The panel does
+not include a general maximum-independent-set or SAT implementation because
+Track B's stronger promise-aware exact algorithm already succeeds; such tools
+could still provide useful comparative timings.
+
+`canonical_key` uses a multiset of affine-invariant pair-pattern signatures. It
+is invariant under group permutation, independent group translations, global
+unit multiplication, reflection, and input reordering, but it is a strong cheap
+invariant rather than a complete graph-isomorphism canonical form and can in
+principle over-collapse contrived pattern assignments. Most importantly, no
+claim should be made from the current error-only oracle transcript. Re-run the
+bare, structural, and placebo harnesses with a usable OpenRouter key and update
+the embedded G9 evidence before emission or submission.
